## Proof

Let $(B(t))_{t \ge 0}$ be a standard Brownian motion. We want to compute
$$E\big[B(t_1)\,B(t_2)\,B(t_3)\big].$$

**Key fact.** A standard Brownian motion is a **Gaussian process**: for any finite collection of times $s_1,\dots,s_n$, the random vector $(B(s_1),\dots,B(s_n))$ is jointly Gaussian (multivariate normal). Moreover, $E[B(t)]=0$ for all $t$, so every such vector is **centered** (zero-mean).

**Odd moments of a centered Gaussian vanish.** Let $X=(X_1,\dots,X_n)$ be a centered jointly Gaussian vector. Its joint characteristic function is
$$\varphi_X(\lambda)=\exp\!\Big(-\tfrac{1}{2}\,\lambda^{\!\top}\Sigma\,\lambda\Big),\qquad \Sigma_{ij}=\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,X_j).$$
This is an even function of $\lambda$ (it depends only on the quadratic form $\lambda^\top\Sigma\lambda$). Expanding $\varphi_X$ as a power series,
$$\varphi_X(\lambda)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{i^k}{k!}\,E\!\Big[(\textstyle\sum_j \lambda_j X_j)^k\Big],$$
the evenness of $\varphi_X$ forces **every odd-order coefficient to be zero**. In particular, every odd moment $E[X_{i_1}X_{i_2}\cdots X_{i_{2m+1}}]=0$.

**Application.** Taking $n=3$ and $(X_1,X_2,X_3)=(B(t_1),B(t_2),B(t_3))$, which is a centered jointly Gaussian vector, the third-order moment is an odd moment, hence
$$E\big[B(t_1)\,B(t_2)\,B(t_3)\big]=0.$$

**Direct verification via independence (alternative).** Assume WLOG $t_1\le t_2\le t_3$. Using independent increments of Brownian motion, write
$$B(t_1)=Z_1,\quad B(t_2)=Z_1+Z_2,\quad B(t_3)=Z_1+Z_2+Z_3,$$
where $Z_1=B(t_1)$, $Z_2=B(t_2)-B(t_1)$, $Z_3=B(t_3)-B(t_2)$ are **independent, centered Gaussians**. Then
$$B(t_1)B(t_2)B(t_3)=Z_1(Z_1+Z_2)(Z_1+Z_2+Z_3).$$
Expanding and taking expectations, every term contains at least one $Z_i$ raised to the first power (an odd, mean-zero factor) times independent factors, so by independence every term has expectation $0$. For instance the term $Z_1^2 Z_3$ gives $E[Z_1^2]\,E[Z_3]=0$, the term $Z_1 Z_2^2$ gives $E[Z_1]\,E[Z_2^2]=0$, etc. Summing all such terms yields $0$.

Both arguments give the same conclusion.

$$\boxed{E\big[B(t_1)\,B(t_2)\,B(t_3)\big]=0.}$$

